protesters defy kovid restrictions  banning protests to gather in their  thousands in central tunis  their angry president kai side has  dissolved tunisia's judicial watchdog  the supreme judicial council and his  latest law gives him near total control  over the appointment and dismissal of  judges  critics say he's now consolidated power  over all the country's institutions  the president has relieved some judges  of their posts dismissed others and  prevented them from objecting or  striking the judiciary that we resort to  is now under the control of the  president  g7 states in the un have voiced deep  concerns about syad's actions and his  increasingly authoritarian rule  the eu says its member states are  discussing cutting funding to tunisia  and tunisia's judges have been on strike  demanding independence for the judiciary  and the reinstatement of the watchdog  kai's side supporters and many lawyers  however support his decision and accused  the council of failing to root out  corruption that they say is rife among  judges  these demonstrators say they're afraid  that kai side is dismantling the state  there are no longer any independent  constitutional institutions today  yesterday was the last of them before he  decided to establish a temporary body  for the supreme judicial council  but a movement calling themselves  citizens against the coup say they're  not giving up  the political and democratic movement of  the street continues and with the same  objectives and the same approach  critics say that kai side is gambling on  gaining total control over tunisia at  the risk of losing the financial support  of the g7 and eu  but this process shows that many  tunisians are prepared to defy his  authority and fight against dictatorship  that they fought so hard to overturn 11  years ago  eliza faltman al jazeera tunis
 

